Instead of calculating every star position from scratch, this keeps the current row width in two values. After printing a row, two stars are removed from the width and one space is added to the left.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern8(int n) {
        int stars = 2 * n - 1;
        int spaces = 0;

        while (stars > 0) {
            for (int i = 0; i < spaces; i++) {
                cout << ' ';
            }
            for (int i = 0; i < stars; i++) {
                cout << '*';
            }
            cout << '\n';

            stars -= 2;
            spaces++;
        }
    }
};
```

The loop stops when the last one-star row has been printed. For `n = 0`, `stars` starts at `-1`, so the method prints nothing.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because the pattern contains that many printed characters.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This version creates each line with repeated characters. `PadLeft` adds the spaces needed to move each shorter row toward the center, while the star count stays odd: `2 * row - 1`.

```csharp
class Solution {
    public void pattern8(int n) {
        for (int row = n; row >= 1; row--) {
            int stars = 2 * row - 1;
            string line = new string('*', stars).PadLeft(stars + n - row);
            System.Console.WriteLine(line);
        }
    }
}
```

The code does not change any input data. It only creates temporary strings for the current row, so a single row never needs more than the width of the pattern.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` to create and print all rows.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the current line.

Each row is made separately with a `StringBuilder`. The first row is the widest, and both the spaces and stars are adjusted as the row number decreases. Building a row first also keeps the printing code easy to follow.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern8(int n) {
        for (int row = n; row >= 1; row--) {
            StringBuilder line = new StringBuilder();

            for (int spaces = 0; spaces < n - row; spaces++) {
                line.append(' ');
            }
            for (int stars = 0; stars < 2 * row - 1; stars++) {
                line.append('*');
            }

            System.out.println(line);
        }
    }
}
```

The `n = 1` case naturally prints one star. If `n` is zero, the loop does not run, so nothing is printed.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the largest row stored in the `StringBuilder`.

The inverted pyramid has `n` rows. For each row, the code prints some leading spaces and then an odd number of stars. The number of stars drops by two after every row.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern8(int n) {
        for (int row = n; row >= 1; row--) {
            int spaces = n - row;
            int stars = 2 * row - 1;

            for (int i = 0; i < spaces; i++) {
                cout << ' ';
            }
            for (int i = 0; i < stars; i++) {
                cout << '*';
            }
            cout << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```

For `n = 3`, the rows contain 5, 3, and 1 stars, so the output is a centered inverted shape.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because all characters in the pattern are printed.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output itself.

